const caseStudies:CaseStudy[]=[{slug:'tile-roof-repair-canberra',title:'Tile Roof Repair Canberra Case Study',summary:'A documented tile-roof work sequence: visible displaced and missing tiles, a controlled work area with tiles lifted, then the visible roof covering reinstated.',servicePath:'/services/tile-roof-repairs',guidePath:'/news/cracked-slipped-missing-roof-tiles-canberra',images:[{src:'/assets/case-studies/tile-roof-repair-before.png',alt:'Canberra tiled roof showing visibly displaced and missing tiles before the documented repair work',caption:'Before: visible displaced and missing tiles were recorded from a safe elevated work position.'},{src:'/assets/case-studies/tile-roof-repair-during.png',alt:'Roofing workers lifting tiles in a controlled work area on a Canberra tiled roof',caption:'During: the local work area was opened while surrounding tiles were set aside for controlled access.'},{src:'/assets/case-studies/tile-roof-repair-after.png',alt:'Canberra tiled roof after the visible work area was retiled',caption:'After: the final image shows the visible work area retiled, with the surrounding weathered roof retained.'}]}];
const serviceSeed:Service[]=[
 {slug:'roof-leak-repairs',title:'Roof Leak Repairs',direct:'A leaking roof is easier to discuss when the visible sign, the weather conditions and the general roof area are recorded separately. Roof leak detection is not a promise that an entry point can be confirmed from an indoor stain or a photo alone; it is a careful assessment pathway for the available evidence.',symptoms:'Note whether the concern is a fresh drip, a damp patch, a ceiling stain, bubbling paint, a musty smell or water near a light fitting. Record the room, when it first appeared and whether it follows steady rain, wind-driven rain or a storm. From ground level, also note nearby tiles, a roof flashing junction, ridge line, chimney, valley or skylight if they are safely visible.',causes:'Water can travel beneath roofing materials, along framing or across a ceiling before it becomes visible indoors. Possible entry areas can include broken or slipped tiles, roof flashing at a junction, penetrations, ridge details or drainage overflow, but a visible mark does not by itself identify which of these is involved.',assessment:'A roof leak assessment begins with the reported water path and the visible roof condition that can be safely reviewed. Conditions that only occur during rain can be harder to assess in dry weather, and access, safety and weather can limit what is observable. If water is near electrical fittings or presents an immediate safety concern, avoid touching the area and seek appropriate urgent assistance.',pathways:'The next step may range from clarifying the concern to discussing a defined repair scope after assessment. If a roof still leaks after a previous repair, the prior work, the timing of the recurrence and the location of the new sign are useful context; they do not establish a cause or warranty outcome by themselves. A temporary measure and a completed repair are not assumed to be the same thing.',scope:'Roof height, access, weather, the location of the reported trace, material condition and what can be safely observed all affect the discussion. We do not promise a fixed diagnosis, repair method, price or response time from an enquiry alone.',faqQ:'Is the water mark always below the leak entry point?',faqA:'Not necessarily. Water can travel along roof elements or ceilings before it becomes visible indoors, so the location of a stain is useful context rather than proof of the entry point.',faqExtras:[{q:'Can roof leak detection be confirmed from a photo?',a:'No. A ground-level photo can help describe the general area, but it cannot confirm concealed flashing, underlay, roof-space conditions or the exact entry point.'},{q:'Can dry weather make a roof leak harder to assess?',a:'Yes. A concern that appears only during rain may leave limited visible evidence in dry weather, so the weather conditions and timing are useful details to include in an enquiry.'},{q:'Can roof flashing cause a leak?',a:'Flashing is one of several roof junction details that may be relevant. The surrounding materials, reported water path and visible condition need to be considered together.'},{q:'What if the roof still leaks after a repair?',a:'Record when the leak returned, the room and weather conditions involved, plus any known previous work. That history helps frame a reassessment enquiry without assuming the cause.'}],next:'Send the location of the visible trace, when it appears, your Canberra suburb and any safe ground-level photos to start an enquiry.'},
 {slug:'tile-roof-repairs',title:'Tile Roof Repairs',direct:'Tile roof repair enquiries are most useful when they begin with what is visible, where it sits on the roofline and whether there are related interior signs. A cracked or slipped tile can be an isolated concern, but it can also sit near a ridge, valley, chimney or roof edge where surrounding details matter.',symptoms:'From a safe ground-level viewpoint, note tiles that look broken, cracked, slipped, missing, displaced or different in colour from nearby tiles. Describe the approximate position: near a ridge line, valley, chimney, roof edge or visible penetration. If the change followed a storm or wind, record when it was first noticed rather than walking on the roof to inspect it closely.',causes:'Visible tile damage can sit alongside weathering, movement, roof interface wear or conditions that are not clear from the ground. A tile that appears damaged is useful information, but it does not by itself show whether there are related materials beneath or around it that need consideration.',assessment:'An assessment may consider accessible tile areas, the surrounding roof surface, valley and ridge interfaces, and safe access requirements. Terracotta and concrete roof tiles can differ in profile, age, finish and weathering. The material type is helpful context, but the suitable approach and any replacement option need to be considered against the actual roof condition.',pathways:'A focused discussion can consider one or two damaged tiles where that is appropriate, together with the visible condition around the area. An individual replacement, a material match or a wider repair cannot be confirmed from a photo alone. Older tile colour, profile and finish can vary, even where a similar tile is available.',scope:'Roof height, safe access, tile availability, material condition, weathering and visible or non-visible conditions around a small defect can affect the scope. This page does not promise an exact match, a particular repair method or replacement of a defined number of tiles before assessment.',faqQ:'Will a replacement tile look exactly the same as an older roof tile?',faqA:'Not necessarily. Existing tile colour and finish can vary with age and weathering, even where a similar option is available.',faqExtras:[{q:'Can one or two damaged tiles be replaced?',a:'A small repair may be considered where appropriate, but the surrounding roof condition, access and availability of suitable materials still need to be assessed.'},{q:'Are terracotta and concrete tile repairs the same?',a:'They can present different profiles, finishes and weathering. The visible material is useful context, but it does not determine a repair method or material match by itself.'},{q:'Should I inspect a broken tile from the roof?',a:'No. Do not climb onto a tiled roof or move tiles to obtain a closer view. A safe ground-level observation and photo are the right starting point for an enquiry.'}],next:'Send the tile location, any related interior sign, your suburb and what you can see safely from the ground.'},
 {slug:'chimney-flashing-repairs',title:'Chimney Flashing Repairs',direct:'Chimney flashing repairs in Canberra start with the roof-to-chimney junction, the location of any interior sign and the weather conditions that make it noticeable. A water mark near a chimney does not prove that the flashing is the sole cause, because several materials and interfaces meet in that area.',symptoms:'Useful observations include water marks on a ceiling or wall near the chimney breast, dampness after wind-driven rain, cracked sealant, old temporary patches, loose adjacent tiles, weathered mortar or visible gaps around the junction. Record whether the concern changes after particular weather and whether the stain is new, recurring or growing.',causes:'The brickwork, chimney, roof covering, flashing, mortar and nearby tiles form a group of connected details. Water can travel before it becomes visible, so an indoor mark may not sit directly beneath the point of entry. Visible sealant or an old patch may be relevant, but it does not identify the full condition of the junction.',assessment:'An assessment may review safely accessible flashing, the chimney junction, adjacent tiles or mortar, and the reported water-entry path. A roof inspection can be a useful starting point where the visible concern is unclear or more than one roof area may be involved. Ground-level photos provide context but cannot show concealed flashing, underlay or the full roof-space condition.',pathways:'Any material choice or repair pathway depends on the site condition, safe access and applicable requirements. This page is specific to the chimney-to-roof junction; it does not claim to cover chimney structural work, fireplace work or every type of roof flashing repair. A broader roof leak pathway may be more appropriate if the reported water sign extends beyond the chimney area.',scope:'Access, roof pitch, chimney junction condition, surrounding material, existing temporary work, weather and the available observations all influence what can be considered. An enquiry does not confirm a cause, a fixed repair method, a price or a response timeframe.',faqQ:'Does cracked sealant identify the whole problem?',faqA:'No. It may be relevant, but the surrounding junction, adjacent tiles or mortar and reported water path also need consideration.',faqExtras:[{q:'Can chimney flashing repairs stop water from wind-driven rain?',a:'Wind-driven rain is useful context, but the visible condition and water path still need assessment before a cause or repair outcome can be identified.'},{q:'Should I use roof leak repairs or chimney flashing repairs?',a:'Choose chimney flashing repairs when the concern is clearly at the chimney-to-roof junction. Choose roof leak repairs when the entry area is uncertain or the signs extend beyond that junction.'},{q:'When is a roof inspection a better starting point?',a:'A roof inspection enquiry can help when you cannot safely identify the relevant roof area, when more than one symptom is present or when you need a visual, non-invasive assessment pathway.'}],next:'Send the chimney location, the room affected, weather conditions, your suburb and any safe ground-level images.'},
 {slug:'rebedding-repointing',title:'Rebedding & Repointing',direct:'Ridge capping work can involve bedding beneath a ridge or hip tile and pointing at its exposed edge. Roof repointing and roof rebedding are related terms, but they describe different parts of the same roof detail and should not be treated as interchangeable.',symptoms:'From the ground, you may notice cracking along a ridge line, loose-looking ridge caps, gaps in visible pointing, weathered material or changes around hip lines. Note whether the concern appears isolated or extends across several roof lines, and whether there is also a ceiling stain or water-entry sign inside the home.',causes:'A surface crack can be limited to visible pointing, or it may sit alongside movement in a cap or bedding that needs closer observation. Age, weathering, roof layout, access and the condition of nearby tiles can all affect the context. A close-up photo can help describe a visible area, but it does not determine the condition beneath a cap.',assessment:'An assessment may consider ridge capping, visible bedding condition, exposed pointing, the way caps are sitting and the relevant tiled roof areas. It may also consider whether a reported interior sign needs to be discussed through the roof leak pathway. No whole-roof conclusion is drawn from a single crack or from an image alone.',pathways:'A local ridge capping repair, roof repointing discussion or a broader approach depends on what is visible across the area and what can be safely assessed. Full repointing is not assumed as a default response, and rebedding is not presumed solely because pointing has surface cracks.',scope:'The extent of cracking, cap stability, roof height, safe access, layout, weather, surrounding tile condition and the available observations can all influence the next step. This page does not guarantee a defined repair scope, material choice, price or outcome before assessment.',faqQ:'Does every crack mean all ridge caps need repointing?',faqA:'No. Surface cracking does not automatically determine a whole-roof approach; local and wider pathways depend on the observed condition.',faqExtras:[{q:'What is the difference between roof rebedding and repointing?',a:'Rebedding concerns the material supporting ridge or hip tiles, while repointing is the exposed finishing material along their edge.'},{q:'Can a loose ridge cap be assessed from the ground?',a:'A ground-level observation can help describe the concern, but it cannot confirm cap stability or the condition of concealed bedding. Do not touch or walk on ridge caps.'},{q:'Should I use roof leak repairs as well?',a:'If there is a ceiling stain, drip or other interior water sign, include that information. The roof leak pathway may help frame the water-entry concern alongside the ridge-line observation.'}],next:'Send the ridge or hip area, what has changed, any interior signs, your suburb and safe ground-level photos.'},
 {slug:'roof-inspections',title:'Roof Inspections',direct:'A roof inspection in Canberra can be a sensible first enquiry when you have a visible concern but cannot safely tell whether it relates to tiles, a roof leak, a chimney junction or a ridge line. It is also useful before deciding what to repair, provided the purpose and the limits of a visual review are clear from the start.',symptoms:'A new water mark, a drip after rain, cracked or slipped tiles seen from the ground, a loose ridge cap, a chimney junction concern or a change near a gutter line can all be useful starting points. Include the property context if it matters, such as a maintenance decision or an upcoming sale, but describe the observable condition rather than asking for a remote diagnosis.',causes:'A visual observation may identify areas for further discussion, but it does not determine every concealed condition or the source of every water mark. Roof materials, weather, access, roof-space visibility and the location of the reported issue can limit what can be seen during an inspection.',assessment:'The scope is visual and non-invasive unless otherwise agreed. Roof-space access, photographs, written notes and any specific focus area are only included where they have been arranged. Safety, weather, access and the agreed purpose can limit observations, and the inspection is not structural engineering, valuation or compliance certification.',pathways:'Observations may help frame a repair or maintenance enquiry and identify whether roof leak repairs, tile roof repairs, chimney flashing repairs or rebedding and repointing is the closest next conversation. A roof inspection does not automatically include a repair quote, report, certification, roof-space access or a guarantee that every hidden condition will be found.',scope:'The agreed purpose, access, safety conditions, weather, roof layout and whether supporting material is arranged affect the scope. Scope and any fees are confirmed before work is arranged; this page does not advertise every inspection or quote as free.',faqQ:'Is a roof inspection a structural or compliance certificate?',faqA:'No. This pathway describes a visual, non-invasive assessment only, not engineering, valuation or certification.',faqExtras:[{q:'Can I arrange a roof inspection before deciding what to repair?',a:'Yes. It can be a useful enquiry where there is a concern but no clear repair category yet. The purpose, access and limits of the visual scope should be agreed first.'},{q:'What should I prepare for a roof inspection enquiry?',a:'Record the suburb, the visible concern, when it was noticed, relevant weather conditions and any safe ground-level photos. Mention any particular area you want to discuss.'},{q:'Does visual and non-invasive mean every roof issue will be found?',a:'No. It means observations are limited to what can be safely and reasonably viewed without invasive investigation, subject to weather and access.'}],next:'Send the reason for the inspection, the visible signs, your Canberra suburb and any particular roof area you would like to describe.'}
];
